{"title":"Indeterminate","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Indeterminate values are an algebraic expression where the limits of the individual parts do not provide enough information to determine the overall limit. In simpler terms, indeterminate values basically tells us that we don't have information yet to know the answer. \n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Example: Consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91d7ada08ad200c068f7060c22ead4ad2e1a2e8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{0}{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91d7ada08ad200c068f7060c22ead4ad2e1a2e8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{0}{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> can equal any value depending on the functions used. Consider the limit: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/4/4c4e166fe8eb7290e480a3a7be6e4eedb8d7be4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{x}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"47\" height=\"34\" />. The limit here is approaching <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. What about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/6/296e2d757b0e7aa9b4ebab06d95ff4b70d5c5533.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{5x}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"56\" height=\"38\" />? Well, the limit here approaches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. These limits are basically telling us how fast each part of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91d7ada08ad200c068f7060c22ead4ad2e1a2e8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{0}{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> is approaching to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> faster. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/8/318595800eea5de11b85a760981557363647b732.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\times \\text{(anything)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"110\" height=\"18\" />, we are \"hiding\" the \"actual\" speed the parts approach <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, thus meaning that the notation doesn't reveal it. So the value exists, but it is not pre-determined. Thus, we say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91d7ada08ad200c068f7060c22ead4ad2e1a2e8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{0}{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> is in indeterminate form.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Note: A common misconception is that \"indeterminate\" and \"undefined\" are the same thing. However, Indeterminate is different than <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Undefined\" title=\"Undefined\">Undefined</a>. Undefined means that there is no mathematical meaning or value. Indeterminate, however, means the value exists, but the current expression doesn't show what it is.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>The most common indeterminate forms are:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/d/91d7ada08ad200c068f7060c22ead4ad2e1a2e8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{0}{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/1/091a15f5eed136bbcf8fc6817c373e6117eb051c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\infty}{\\infty}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"24\" height=\"34\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/0/4a0d8bedb482fcef957a8f2dea1d59883d7af0a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\times \\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/b/cab7c1021a82729ab667edd2f6475d052f33e585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\infty - \\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/0/6b03aef859cd264c09c6ea8cc1f0521a420bb532.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0^0, 1^{\\infty}, \\text{ and } \\infty^0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"20\" /></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018091116\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.008 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.020 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 49/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 2957/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:35824:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018091116 and revision id 278803. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}